/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A driven antenna draws current as if a resistor sat at its terminals, though the power has really left as a wave.
   - Prad = ½ |I|² Rr.
   - The reference point matters because the current varies along an antenna.
   - Short dipoles have two conventions.
   - Metal resistance (raised by the skin effect as √f), dielectric loss and any loading coil are lumped into a loss resistance RL in series with Rr, so ZA = Rr + RL + jXA.
   - ηrad = Rr / (Rr + RL). ηrad is the radiation efficiency, a ratio from 0 to 1
   - Gain follows as G = ηrad D.
   - A DC ohmmeter cannot measure Rr; it exists only at the working frequency, as power that leaves.
   - On receive, an antenna acts as a catching area.
   - Aem = D λ² / 4π.
*/
