/* book exam traps: Quoting Rr without the reference point or the current convention. 80π²(l/λ)² is uniform current; 20π²(l/λ)² is a centre-fed short dipole referred to its feed current. | Using Ae = λ²/4π for every antenna. That is the isotropic value (D = 1); multiply by D, or by G if loss is counted. | Mixing peak and rms current in Prad = ½|I|²Rr. This doubles or halves Rr; the resulting efficiency error depends on RL, so it is not a simple factor of two. | Assuming a resonant antenna is efficient. Resonance only cancels the reactance; efficiency is set by Rr against RL. | Calling AR = 1 linear. AR = 1 is circular; a linear wave has AR → ∞, and a linear antenna receiving a circular wave loses 3 dB. */
