/* book exam traps: Reading D in 2D²/λ as directivity. It is the largest physical dimension in metres. | Applying 2D²/λ to an electrically small antenna. When it gives less than a wavelength, the wavelength rule (about 1.6λ here) decides. | Thinking that a higher frequency shortens the far-field distance. For a fixed D, 2D²/λ grows as f grows: a dish needs a longer range at a higher frequency. | Taking E/H = 377 Ω everywhere. In the reactive region of a short dipole it can be thousands of ohms. | Thinking the far field is where the signal is strong. The boundary is about phase flatness and a fixed pattern, not about signal level. */
